spoint physics-engine evaluation VERDICT (user asked: Havok or Babylon.js a step up?). DECLINE both, KEEP Jolt+three. Reasons: (1) Babylon.js as a whole engine would REPLACE three.js -> rewrites the ENTIRE spoint client (three-vrm players, streaming-gltf ModelPool, mapspinner WebGL2 planet rendered INTO three's GL context, @three.ez InstancedMesh2 foliage, octahedral-impostor, editor + scene-graph + all integrations) = the maximal heavy-rewrite that NET-GROWS code and discards the VRM + mapspinner ecosystem spoint depends on. (2) Havok physics standalone: its web build (@babylonjs/havok HavokPhysics.wasm) is consumed THROUGH Babylon's Physics V2 plugin (havokPlugin.ts) - using it without Babylon means reimplementing that plugin against the raw undocumented HK WASM API; and Jolt is ALREADY a top-tier WASM physics engine (same quality tier), so Havok would neither reduce code nor meaningfully improve speed, while incurring a large risky full-physics-layer migration (World.js + CharacterController + all body mgmt + raycasts + SDK physics). (3) Neither beats the targeted fix for the ACTUAL problem (tree/rock collider streaming hitch = create/destroy churn, which is engine-agnostic): BODY POOLING (pre-create bodies per limited shapeKey, stream by REPOSITION via bodyInterface.SetPositionAndRotation + Activate/Deactivate instead of create/destroy). This matches the foliage-physics-fusion workflow's crashcat-decline reasoning. Decision: keep Jolt, implement body pooling.